Z||000000||Sootah||05-15-2001||12:37 AM||||Which repair manual should I get?||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroLisa||05-15-2001||12:43 AM||||I have both Chiltons and Haynes. Haynes is simpler, but doesn't have a lot of stuff- Chiltons takes some knowledge to read, but its got lots more info... I would go for Chiltons<P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 violet pearl 4 speed GT: 17 in. rims, lowered, cold-air intake<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48495967&Sequence=0&res=high"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-15-2001||01:21 AM||||The Factory Service Manual has EVERYTHING. Haynes is my second choice. They cover almost as much as the factory one. Chilton's is OK. I have seen many errors and some stuff missing in the Chilton's books. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||05-15-2001||12:26 PM||||i noticed when i got a new haynes recently that the electrical diagrams have been simplified some and aren't as specific bettween years and engines. wonder why.<P>seems like haynes are easier to find but if i come across others i try to get them too. sometimes procedures get explained better in one or another of them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steven||05-15-2001||01:37 PM||||I use the '84, '85, & '87 factory service manuals, togather with the Hayne's manual, and in doing my engine rebuild, I have be using my "how to rebuild your GM v-6 60* engine". Together these books have been very helpful.<P>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||05-15-2001||05:39 PM||||My Chilton's is the old style with the white cover (I bought it about 10 years ago), dunno if the newer green ones have the same content or not but I prefer the Hayes.<P>IMO the Hayes and the factory Helms (http://www.helminc.com) manual for your year are the way to go.  I found the exploded diagrams in the 22P very useful a couple of times as well but I'd get a Hayes and a Helms first then go from there. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||beachbomb||05-15-2001||06:42 PM||||I have both the Factory service manual and Haynes, Haynes says it in simple terms, to be honest i use both and compare the two, works for me!! By the way, Fierolisa, i'll trade my Indy for that GT!!!!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">..Jim(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||1
Z||000007||kslish||05-16-2001||03:09 AM||||You could always be like me and collect them all.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I've got:<P>1.) The old Chilton's (white cover and has a red '88 Formula on the front)<P>2.) The new Chilton's (the Total Car Care one, much bigger with a white '85 GT on the front)  Seems to be the old one redone with a lot more drawings and diagrams.  Has a section of wiring and vacuum digrams that the old one lacked.<P>3.) the Haynes (I use this the most).  I recommend this one if you are only looking to buy one.<P>4.) a Clymer manual.  This one is small like the first Chilton's and only covers the 1984-1986 model year.<P>5.) The 22P parts book.  Not really a repair manual but helps with part ordering and I use the excellent diagrams in it to figure out how everything goes back together after tearing it all apart.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Still looking for some economical copies of the factory manuals.  Why do I have all of these?  Some manuals explain some things better and it's always good to have a second opinion.<P>You can check out pics and info on all of the manuals at the following page I made:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/wpfc/manuals.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/wpfc/manuals.html</A> <P>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
